# VITE + REACT + THREE.JS LIVE SAFETY (CRITICAL)

The user is frequently play-testing the 3D dice game live in the browser (localhost:5173). Full page reloads destroy progress, selected skins, and debugging state. These rules have highest priority.

## 1. Never force a full page reload while the user is testing
- Prefer the smallest possible change that Vite can hot-update.
- Do not restart the Vite dev server or kill processes.
- Avoid changes that touch the root entry point, main Three.js scene setup, or files that Vite treats as non-HMR-safe unless absolutely necessary.
- If a change will cause a full reload, **warn the user first** and ask for confirmation.

## 2. Three.js scene & renderer cleanup
- When editing the dice rendering code, always ensure the old renderer, scene, camera, and animation loop are properly disposed on HMR.
- Prefer updating materials, textures, or dice meshes in place instead of recreating the entire scene when possible.
- Never leave orphaned WebGL contexts or multiple canvases.

## 3. Skins / sprites / assets (highest priority)
- Skins and dice textures live in the project assets and are referenced from code + stored in localStorage via `src/lib/localProfile.js`.
- **NEVER overwrite, regenerate, delete, or “improve” existing skin/texture image files** unless the user explicitly names the exact file and confirms.
- Only change the code that *selects* or *applies* the skin (the mapping, the material assignment, the localStorage key, etc.).
- If a new skin variant is needed, create a brand-new file. Do not modify existing ones.
- After any skin-related change, remind the user that localStorage may still hold the previous selection and how to force a refresh of the skin if needed.

## 4. Persistence awareness
- Player progress (coins, XP, selected skins, felt tuning) is stored in localStorage through `src/lib/localProfile.js`.
- Do not clear, overwrite, or reset localStorage keys unless the user explicitly asks to reset progress.
- When debugging skin application, check both the code path and what is currently saved in localStorage.

## 5. When the user says “it refreshed and everything reset” or “my skins are gone”
- Treat it as a high-priority failure.
- Investigate whether the last edit forced a full reload or touched an asset file.
- Fix the root cause before doing any other work.
- Summarize exactly which files were changed and whether a full reload is expected.

These rules override normal agent behavior. Violating them wastes the user’s live testing progress.
